ethiopia has begun filling the  reservoirs of a controversial mega dam  on the blue nile that's according to the  country's water minister  egypt and sudan had requested the grand  renaissance dam  not be filled until an agreement is  reached there's been nearly a decade of  contentious negotiation over concerns  it could drastically reduce water  supplies to downstream countries  ethiopia has said the dam is crucial for  its growth  and will begin filling it during this  month's rainy season  charles stratford lays out the politics  behind the dam  it's taken ten years to build and cost  close to five billion dollars  ethiopia's flagship infrastructure  project is a great source of national  pride but the grand ethiopian  renaissance dam on the blue nile has  caused tension with downstream countries  sudan and egypt  from the start the ethiopian government  says the hydroelectric dam is vital for  the development of this immense  landlocked country which has a  population of around 108 million  the country plans to export electricity  across the region  and says the dam will stop seasonal  flooding in neighboring sudan  but egypt says there are a number of  outstanding legal issues that need  resolving  and filling the reservoir too quickly  could reduce its water supply  and may pose an existential threat egypt  does have legitimate concerns i mean the  egypt relies  on denial for 90 percent of its fresh  water needs  uh and the largest share of the river  runs through egypt through egyptian  territory  uh and what we have as in egypt is a  situation where they have a  significant gap between the amount of  water they produce  and the amount of water they consume  and with a rapidly growing population of  over 100 million  it points to this problem only getting  worse  ethiopia also has strong claims because  it's the source of the blue nile  the largest of the river niles  tributaries ethiopia was never included  in a colonial era agreement between  egypt and sudan  that gives the lion's share of water to  egypt  there are many finely balanced  calculations including internal  political ones that are a play here  you know for example in ethiopia you  have abi ahmed having come into power in  2018  he wants to secure his position and and  you know be re-elected in future  elections in ethiopia  in egypt you have president sisi with  you know an economy that has  had been struggling but also he needs to  ensure  that he has the continued backing of the  military establishment in the country  which of course has an important role in  that  economy in late june ethiopia rejected a  u.n security council meeting requested  by egypt  saying the council should not be  involved at all  ethiopia's u.n ambassador said the  dispute should be solved at a regional  level  african union talks hosted by south  africa failed to forge agreement earlier  that month  in february the u.s and the world bank  mediated what it hoped would be a road  map on how to proceed with final  technical and legal issues which  concerned egypt in particular  but ethiopia did not sign the final  document  ethiopia always said that it would have  preferred agreement  before it begins to fill the reservoir  but if that wasn't possible it would go  ahead anyway  it has potential huge security  implications for the entire region  ethiopia accuses egypt of repeatedly  dragging its feet over the issue of  filling the reservoir  something it says egypt knew would be  inevitable  one day charles stratford al jazeera  our correspondent hippo morgan's joining  us live from now from northern state in  sudan um do we have any idea about what  uh sudan's reaction is going to be  well rob we're yet to hear a new  reaction  or any statement from the chinese  government uh we've heard from them  yesterday saying that  uh they are going to present a report to  the uh  african union they've already submitted  reports from the two weeks  of talks of virtual talks between  ethiopia sudan and egypt  to their prime ministers and uh there is  supposed to be a summit that is uh  going to take place between the prime  ministers of the three countries  within the coming week now this was all  to try to make sure that  appeal is reached before ethiopia starts  filling its grand dam now this is  obviously  a little bit too late for that uh there  have been repeated concerns from sudan  about the safety of the operations of  the dam  let's not forget that sudan itself has  another dam that's about 100 kilometers  away  from the girl the grand island  renaissance dam which is called the  ferris  so it's quite concerned about what would  happen when the dam fails to operate  properly  and fails to hold water and all of a  sudden all that water  that has been held by the reservoir gets  released downstream all of them will not  be able to handle repairs itself  has the capacity of seven thousand  that's uh  that's about ten times less less than  the uh grand  dam of ethiopia so lots of concerns  there egypt of course we know has been  uh very concerned about the killing of  the dam because that will affect  the water levels it has uh which more  than 90 percent of its population relies  on so  lots of concerns there were yet to hear  reactions from the vietnamese government  they've repeatedly said that there  should be a diplomatic solution  and that there should be a political  will to reach a deal which is why  they've been trying to meditate between  the two sides while saying that it at  the same time is a stakeholder  so lots of concerns here about how to  handle the coming three steps  over the coming few days now that the  first step of the dam has actually  started  i would understand that um people living  in and around the dam are also going to  be concerned about  uh safety issues uh the the same safety  issues that sudan has  is there any effort that sudan can make  at least in governmental terms to try to  protect people who might be affected  well as you heard from my colleagues uh  when he  reported just a few minutes ago that uh  if you have said that the dam  will control seasonal flooding uh along  the nile in sudan that's something that  sedan has repeatedly suffered from  especially during the rainy season  flooding uh along the banks of the nile  as the nile flows from  uh the country towards egypt now the dam  will control that however  that is what farmers say uh they mean  we've spoken to farmers along the nile  from pronounced state  uh on the border with if you're here all  the way up to uh alpha  uh on the border with egypt they say  that they need that uh  that motion of the flooding uh from the  nile uh to be able to uh have  a more fertile soil to be able to uh  irrigate their plants  properly so while we have heard people  uh complaining about the floods over the  past few  years they say that those floods that  have been  uh happening uh recurring uh annually is  very important  so that they would be able to uh  continue farming and continue to  continue producing there are a lot of  farmland  agricultural profound land along the  banks of the mile as we go down uh  northwards from uh from sudan borders  going on to egypt so lots of concerns by  the farmers  about what will happen when the water  levels drop on the nile  tiber morgan reporting to us from sudan  hibba thanks very much indeed  alice katopoulos has more on where the  concerns lie for egypt and sudan  ethiopia's largest hydroelectric project  has been controversial since its  beginning  in 2011. but what is it about the grand  ethiopian renaissance dam  that has egypt and sudan so concerned  let's start with what it can do for  ethiopia itself  a country of more than 109 million  people its economy is one of the fastest  growing in the world  at roughly 10 a year for the last 15  years  ethiopia's energy demands have kept pace  but only 27 percent of the country is  currently connected to the national grid  this dam the largest in africa spans the  blue nile  and will produce a whopping 6450  gigawatts when it's fully operational  three times as much as egypt's has one  high dam giving ethiopia the ability  to connect every single citizen to the  electricity grid  drive its economy and still have energy  left over to export to its neighbors  sudan djibouti somalia and kenya  so far so good then what is the issue  a big dam needs a big reservoir and this  one will be huge  at 1 840 square kilometers  it will hold more than two and a half  times the amount of water in lake  tana ethiopia's largest lake and the  source of the blue nile  the dam will take years to fill and the  fear is it will reduce the water flowing  downstream to sudan and egypt  currently 86 of the blue nile flows from  ethiopia  the river provides 77 of fresh water for  sudan  and 93 of egypt's if there is a  reduction in the water flowing through  these desert countries  it could have a serious impact on  agriculture and livelihoods  other hydroelectric projects like  egypt's as one high dam  currently producing six percent of  egypt's electricity would also be  greatly affected  egypt and sudan's populations are  rapidly growing and within the resource  needs but for ethiopia  the 4 billion dam is a way out of  poverty  and an end to decades of rolling  blackouts with global warming and  changing weather patterns  managing scarce resources is becoming  increasingly vital alex skatopoulos  al jazeera

